# Paper running example (Asma et al., NPCS, WWW'24 — Example 3.2), encoded with
# NAMED-GRAPH reification: each base triple lives in its OWN named graph, and the
# graph name IS the provenance token u_i (the IRI NPCS's namedgraph scheme matches,
# emitted as GRAPH ?prov { s p o }). Tokens are the SAME ex:u_i as the Standard and
# RDF-star encodings, so the emitted circuit is byte-identical across all three.
#
# Use with:  java -cp target/npcs-rewrite.jar npcs.circuit.CircuitRun NamedGraph \
#              examples/data/example.namedgraph.nq <query.sparql>

# --- Example 3.2 (verbatim): Alice ---
<http://example.org/paper#Alice> <http://example.org/paper#likes> <http://example.org/paper#pasta> <http://example.org/paper#u1> .
<http://example.org/paper#Alice> <http://example.org/paper#likes> <http://example.org/paper#pasta> <http://example.org/paper#u2> .
<http://example.org/paper#Alice> <http://example.org/paper#livesIn> <http://example.org/paper#Italy> <http://example.org/paper#u3> .

# --- Added so UNION / MINUS / OPTIONAL return non-trivial provenance ---
<http://example.org/paper#Bob> <http://example.org/paper#likes> <http://example.org/paper#pasta> <http://example.org/paper#u4> .
<http://example.org/paper#Carol> <http://example.org/paper#livesIn> <http://example.org/paper#Italy> <http://example.org/paper#u5> .
